      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"randomly hunting\" or \"hunting\" behavior can be attributed to the uneven heat distribution within the brake system, particularly in high-speed applications. When heat builds up within the brake pads, brake calipers, or master cylinder, it can cause thermal expansion and contraction of the steered wheel or wheel bearings, leading to:\n\n1. Unbalanced brake pressure: Heat-induced material expansion or contraction of brake components (e.g., caliper piston, wheel bearings) can become uneven, creating variations in brake pressure between the wheels, leading to uneven braking dynamics.\n2. Dynamic caliper critique: Friction-induced heat buildup can result in oscillations between caliper-specific thermal stress states.\n\nIn high-speed descents, these thermal variations can induce oscillations that manifest as the 'hunting' behavior you've described. This 'hunting' can cause the steering to wander around the centerline, making the bike or vehicle more difficult to control.",
